      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we'll start by determining the ratio of the perimeters of the two similar triangles. The ratio of the perimeters will be the same as the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nFirst, we find the perimeter of the original triangle. The sides of the triangle are 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm. The perimeter \\( P \\) is given by:\n\\[\nP = 6 + 7 + 9 = 22 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThe perimeter of the similar triangle is given as 110 cm. Let's denote the perimeter of the similar triangle as \\( P_{\\text{similar}} \\). The ratio of the perimeters of the two triangles is:\n\\[\n\\frac{P_{\\text{similar}}}{P} = \\frac{110}{22} = 5\n\\]\nThis means that each side of the similar triangle is 5 times the corresponding side of the original triangle.\n\nNow, we need to find the length of the longest side of the similar triangle. The longest side of the original triangle is 9 cm. According to the ratio, the longest side of the similar triangle will be:\n\\[\n9 \\times 5 = 45 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{45}\n\\]",
